WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.507 --> 00:00:04.507
(indistinct background chatter)

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.460
- I think we're about ready
to go and get started.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:13.610
As everyone continues to take their seat,

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:14.443
I want to welcome you.

00:00:14.443 --> 00:00:15.920
Good afternoon, everyone,

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.210
I'm Kim Goff-Crews,

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:21.450
and it is my privilege today
to welcome you to our ceremony

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:23.560
as we honor our veterans.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:26.037
As we gathered together
with President Salovey,

00:00:26.037 --> 00:00:29.300
Provost Strobel, officers
of the university,

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:32.450
the commanding officers of
our Naval and Air Force,

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.440
Reserve Officers Training Corps,

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.240
and all of you, our honored guests,

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:39.940
I'm also pleased to
extend a special welcome

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.240
to Brigadier General Donald
Singer and his wife Anne.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:45.694
Welcome, thank you for being here.

00:00:45.694 --> 00:00:48.194
(crowd claps)

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:51.820
Before we begin, I would also like

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:53.608
to thank the Yale Police
Department Color Guard

00:00:53.608 --> 00:00:56.050
for the opening ceremony,
thank you very much.

00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:59.120
And to share our land
acknowledgement, which states,

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:00.240
the university acknowledges

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.350
the indigenous peoples and nations,

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:05.010
including Mohegan, Mashantucket Pequot,

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:10.010
Eastern Pequot, Schaghticoke,
Golden Hill Paugussett,

00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:11.990
Niantic, and the Quinnipiac

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:14.430
and other Algonquian speaking peoples,

00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.170
who have stewarded through generations

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.410
the lands and waterways

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:19.940
of what is now the State of Connecticut.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.570
We honor and respect the
enduring relationship

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:26.063
that exists between these peoples
and nations and this land.

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.750
So what a pleasure to be
with all of you outside

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:33.750
and in front of this
beautifully restored cenotaph

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:35.310
and university flagpole,

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:40.200
for the return of an official
ceremony to Hewitt Quadrangle.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.030
The last two years have been
challenging, to say the least,

00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:46.340
with understandable limits on
how we can gather together.

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.109
But somehow it feels fitting

00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:50.810
that one of our first larger events

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.150
is one in which we can publicly recognize

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.510
the important contributions of veterans

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.010
to the life of the university.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.830
We have with us today,
students, faculty, and staff,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:06.530
and alumni who have served or
who are preparing to serve.

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.200
And we are grateful for
the freedoms and privileges

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.383
that their sacrifices have made possible.

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:15.280
During our ceremony today, as
you'll see on your program,

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:18.320
you will hear from a veteran
leader, a current student,

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.123
and we will also honor
a 47-year staff person,

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:28.300
not 47 years old, but
been here for 47 years.

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.260
And she supported most of that time

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:32.053
and advocated for Yale Veterans.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:36.040
Our speakers and honoree represent

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.570
commitment to honor and country,

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:42.410
and to veterans across multiple
generations and conflicts.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:45.300
They represent all the veterans
who are here with us today

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.100
and those who care and support them.

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.150
It is my pleasure now to
introduce President Peter Salovey,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:55.330
who will share his thoughts
on our treasured veterans.

00:02:55.330 --> 00:02:58.490
President Salovey.
(crowd claps)

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.110
- Thank you, Vice President Goff-Crews

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:07.293
Hello everyone.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:09.960
Thank you for being here.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.960
Thank you for being here to
honor veterans past and present.

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.180
It's wonderful to be together.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.470
It's wonderful to be in person together

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:24.153
to be on campus and to
recognize Veterans Day.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.760
So we're joined today by
faculty, staff, students,

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.040
and alumni who are veterans,

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.530
as well as students who currently serve

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:34.810
in the Reserve Officers Training Corps

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.410
here on Yale's campus,

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.040
to all of our current and former members

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:43.203
of the armed forces, thank you.

00:03:43.203 --> 00:03:46.743
With our very deepest
gratitude and respect.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.850
Yale students, alumni, faculty and staff

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.230
have served their country honorably

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.820
for over three centuries.

00:03:55.820 --> 00:03:57.580
We're proud of them.

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:01.140
We're proud of the rich legacy of service

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.253
that they represent.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.930
We think of Nathan Hale,
Yale College class of 1773,

00:04:08.620 --> 00:04:11.190
perhaps the most famous alumnus

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.683
to serve in the American Revolution.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.830
We think of Cortlandt
Van Rensselaer Creed,

00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:19.290
the first African American

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:22.300
to earn a medical degree from Yale.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.270
Who in spite of tremendous
racism and discrimination

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:29.063
served in the Union Army
during the Civil War.

00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:33.870
And we think of Rear Admiral Grace Hopper,

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:38.010
who received her master's
and PhD in mathematics

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.130
from Yale University.

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:45.010
When she retired from the
Navy, at the age of 79,

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:49.393
she was the oldest serving
officer in the U.S. Armed Forces.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.750
Remembering the service
and sacrifice of veterans

00:04:53.750 --> 00:04:56.913
is a responsibility we all share.

00:04:57.980 --> 00:05:02.180
100 years ago on November 11th, 1921,

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.700
the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.810
was dedicated in Arlington
National Cemetery,

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.150
as a place to honor and commemorate

00:05:09.150 --> 00:05:12.570
American lives lost in war.

00:05:12.570 --> 00:05:16.883
And Yale too shares in this history.

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:20.510
Ferdinand Foch, a French military leader,

00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:22.170
who served as commander in chief

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.640
of the Allied Armies in World War I,

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:29.410
spoke at Arlington at the
funeral for The Unknown Soldier,

00:05:29.410 --> 00:05:33.030
and the next day traveled to New Haven

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.943
where he received an
honorary degree from Yale.

00:05:37.900 --> 00:05:42.900
Our university knew the terrible
cost of war all too well.

00:05:43.770 --> 00:05:46.620
Here on Hewitt Quadrangle,

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:51.620
the Memorial Cenotaph honors
225 Yale's students and alumni

00:05:52.890 --> 00:05:54.380
who died in the Great War

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:59.200
and carved into the
walls of Memorial Hall.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:04.200
You can find the names of
1,020 alumni killed in battle

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:06.363
since the American Revolution.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:10.390
Yale's tradition of
military service continues

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:13.683
with the students who
currently serve in Yale ROTC.

00:06:14.710 --> 00:06:17.670
And this year we are particularly grateful

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:21.060
for their example of service.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:26.060
The pandemic has reminded
us time and time again,

00:06:26.070 --> 00:06:29.760
of the critical importance of
putting the needs of others

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:31.323
above our own.

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:36.483
Service is part of the Yale
veteran communities DNA.

00:06:37.400 --> 00:06:41.000
We see that in the work of
the Yale Veterans Network,

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:44.430
a campus group for students,
faculty, and staff,

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:48.533
and the Yale Veterans Association
and alumni organization.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.000
In the early days of the pandemic,

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:53.300
the Yale Veterans Network

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:56.400
supported the downtown
evening soup kitchen

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.763
helping provide meals to
our neighbors in New Haven.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:02.910
Last winter, they
continued their tradition

00:07:02.910 --> 00:07:04.030
of collecting toys

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:07.930
for the U.S. Marines
Toys for Tots Program,

00:07:07.930 --> 00:07:11.203
which provides gifts for
children at the holidays.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:15.620
The new co-chairs of the
Yale Veterans Network,

00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:18.640
Shawn Flynn and Tamika Hollis,

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:22.530
are continuing to support
veterans within the Yale community

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:26.610
while also helping those in
need in the broader community.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:31.330
I thank them for their service
as we honor the contributions

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:34.193
and sacrifices of all veterans.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:39.283
At the going down of the
sun and in the morning,

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:41.473
we will remember them.

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.500
Thank you very much,
thank you for being here.

00:07:46.881 --> 00:07:49.998
(crowd claps)
- Atten-hut!

00:07:49.998 --> 00:07:50.998
Ready, halt!

00:07:58.991 --> 00:08:02.408
("Star Spangled Banner")

00:09:21.023 --> 00:09:22.023
Order, halt!

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:28.543
Ready, two!

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:38.670
- Grand architect of the universe

00:09:38.670 --> 00:09:40.360
called by many names

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:43.350
in many languages by many voices.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:46.840
Today, we ask that your
guiding hands hold us,

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:50.620
and all sisters and brothers
in military service near.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:55.620
We are grateful for all who
serve, all who have served.

00:09:56.390 --> 00:09:57.300
And for those who,

00:09:57.300 --> 00:09:59.747
in the words of President Abraham Lincoln

00:09:59.747 --> 00:10:02.357
"Gave the last full measure of devotion

00:10:02.357 --> 00:10:04.077
"and service to their country."

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:08.100
We are joined in humility, gratitude,

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:10.390
and our own measure of devotion,

00:10:10.390 --> 00:10:13.260
knowing that as we seek to understand

00:10:13.260 --> 00:10:16.260
an increasingly complex world,

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:18.450
we are sheltered by the vigilant service

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:20.363
of citizens to our Republic.

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:25.200
We ask special regard for
those who have served,

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.623
but who have not yet been
found and returned home.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:31.900
And for those who have returned home,

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:35.023
but who have yet to
find themselves at home.

00:10:36.360 --> 00:10:39.570
We honor our military and ask that

00:10:39.570 --> 00:10:41.170
whatever is required of them,

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:44.140
be informed by your infinite wisdom,

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:48.450
the light and the truth as
they honorably and loyally

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:52.697
seek to create a peaceful
and secure future for us all.

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:13.210
- Good afternoon, everyone.

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:14.110
First and foremost,

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:15.580
I'd like to give recognition and thanks

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:18.300
to President Salovey,
Secretary Goff-Crews,

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.810
Provost Strobel, Chaplain Kugler,

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:22.400
Tamika Hollis, Valerie Stanley,

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:24.360
and the Veterans Day committee.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:25.420
I'd also like to personally thank

00:11:25.420 --> 00:11:26.800
Dean Risa Sodi.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:29.960
I'd like to thank head David Evans,

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.950
Dean Brianna Bilski and Damaris
Cardona of Berkeley College

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:35.600
for their constant support
over the last three years.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.100
I'd also get to take a moment to recognize

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.270
and thank the men and women in uniform

00:11:40.270 --> 00:11:43.280
and those in civil
service, law enforcement

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:44.850
and the fire department.

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:48.050
Last but not least, I wanna
thank my incredible parents

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:51.100
who are here in the front row
or second row right before me,

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:52.803
my father Tai and my mother Anh.

00:11:54.030 --> 00:11:56.513
It is-
(crowd claps)

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:02.940
It was Veterans Day and
we're thanking our veterans,

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:05.340
but it's veteran families
who endure more hardship

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:06.420
than you could imagine.

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:08.740
They sit back and constant worry and fear

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.210
while we across the world during our job,

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:11.310
doing what we love,

00:12:11.310 --> 00:12:14.100
and they stay in a state
of unrest and concern

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:17.000
not knowing the danger we
are in and what could happen.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:19.610
To all in attendance,
thank you for coming.

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:21.460
It's an honor to be here
with you all on this day

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:24.400
to thank our veterans to
honor their sacrifices.

00:12:24.400 --> 00:12:28.400
October 19th, 2001, the U.S.
Army 75th Ranger Regiment,

00:12:28.400 --> 00:12:31.930
as part of a Special
Operations Joint Task Force,

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:35.420
parachute dropped in
(mumbles) in Afghanistan.

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:36.979
Army Rangers were then-

00:12:36.979 --> 00:12:37.930
(clears throat) Excuse me,

00:12:37.930 --> 00:12:40.230
continue to deploy to combat ever since.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:43.420
Up until recently, that's
well over 7,000 days

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:45.310
of unbroken combat operations.

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:48.810
During this time 52 Army Rangers
gave the ultimate sacrifice

00:12:48.810 --> 00:12:49.780
laying down their lives,

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:51.793
over 450 were wounded in action.

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:54.690
Many care, the physical wounds of war,

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:57.000
almost all carry the invisible scars.

00:12:57.000 --> 00:12:58.770
They joined the service whole

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:02.170
and often leave pieces of
themselves in these distant lands.

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:04.670
Yet they would never leave
a fallen brother behind

00:13:04.670 --> 00:13:05.503
and they will drive on

00:13:05.503 --> 00:13:07.970
to complete the mission no matter what.

00:13:07.970 --> 00:13:09.370
An Army Ranger chooses this,

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:11.210
he volunteers for it,

00:13:11.210 --> 00:13:13.860
fully knowing the hazards
of his chosen profession.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.320
He accepts the fact as the ranger,

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:17.610
his country expects him to move further,

00:13:17.610 --> 00:13:20.260
faster than fight harder
than any other soldier.

00:13:20.260 --> 00:13:23.050
Arranger will shoulder more
than his share of the task

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:24.493
100% and then some.

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:27.590
It's not for the money,
he makes not much more

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:31.540
than if he chose a regular
9 to 5 non-combat job.

00:13:31.540 --> 00:13:33.090
It's not for the benefits
you get the same,

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:34.890
if you chose a less hazardous specialty

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:37.430
with a shorter enlistment contract.

00:13:37.430 --> 00:13:38.740
It's not for the work-life balance,

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:41.540
he trains for 7 months and
deploys 4 months every year,

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:44.280
giving up time with his
family and loved ones.

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:45.113
So why does he do it?

00:13:45.113 --> 00:13:47.070
He does it for the man
to his left and his right

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:49.410
to protect his loved ones back home,

00:13:49.410 --> 00:13:52.690
for all of us civilians
stateside right now,

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:54.150
regardless of whether
you support him or not,

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:56.030
he's willing to sacrifice
everything for you.

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:58.300
Many have given the ultimate sacrifice

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:00.050
and may they never be forgotten.

00:14:00.050 --> 00:14:02.360
I'm proud of serving
the 75th Ranger Regiment

00:14:02.360 --> 00:14:04.060
proud to have deployed
twice to Afghanistan

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:05.640
with 1st Ranger Battalion.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:07.130
Lucky to be standing
here with you all today

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:10.330
and grateful to all
veterans before and after me

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:13.610
who have answered the call
with selfless service.

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:15.760
My motivation to join the military

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:17.530
was rooted in my parents' sacrifice,

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:18.640
to leave their homes in Vietnam

00:14:18.640 --> 00:14:20.710
in search of a better
life for their families.

00:14:20.710 --> 00:14:22.070
They were just kids.

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:23.580
My father was a teenager,

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:25.330
with no other choice would
have to leave his own father

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.060
and everything he knew behind

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:29.760
to embark on a dangerous
journey to some far off land.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:32.140
This far off land was the United States,

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:34.330
and it gave my family an
opportunity at a better life,

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:36.740
the chance to give back
to this great nation

00:14:36.740 --> 00:14:40.080
that welcomed my family
and gave them opportunity

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:41.610
to protect the freedom that my parents

00:14:41.610 --> 00:14:42.770
risked their lives to have

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.690
for me, that alone was worth fighting for.

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:48.700
I remember when I finished the
first phase of Ranger School,

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:51.000
I'd lost about 15 pounds in 20 days

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:52.940
and had essentially been
drained of all my strength.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:55.380
When I pitifully jogged out of the gates

00:14:55.380 --> 00:14:58.410
of the training compound
for my 8 hour break or pass,

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:00.190
I was in shock to see my parents,

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:03.280
who had driven down to Georgia
from New York to surprise me.

00:15:03.280 --> 00:15:04.870
Due to exhaustion I don't remember much

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:06.120
of those next 8 hours,

00:15:06.120 --> 00:15:09.100
but I do remember my mother
and father caring for me,

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:10.900
all my fingers were infected and swollen

00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:13.180
to the point of immobility,

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:14.730
but I do remember coming
in and out of sleep

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.560
to my mother cleaning each
of my fingers and nails.

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:18.740
I remember sitting at a buffet

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:21.560
right before having to go
back to the training compound

00:15:21.560 --> 00:15:23.050
and seeing the looks on my parents' faces

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:23.910
as they watch me struggle

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:26.880
to simply lift the glass
of water to my mouth.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:28.080
The next two weeks in Ranger School

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:30.720
were among the most physically
difficult in my life.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:33.120
I'd essentially lost almost
all strength and control

00:15:33.120 --> 00:15:34.670
on my arms and fingers.

00:15:34.670 --> 00:15:38.580
I tore and re-tore the ligaments
in my ankles repeatedly

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:42.170
only for me to be supported
by a tight tie of the boots.

00:15:42.170 --> 00:15:43.440
Yet everyday I continued to get

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:45.210
that 90-pound rucksack on my back

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:46.980
and just did what I had to do,

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:49.750
to be a contributing member on my team.

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:51.870
So what's the lesson here?

00:15:51.870 --> 00:15:54.200
Well, in hindsight, if
you're in that condition,

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:56.613
it's highly recommended
to go see a physician.

00:15:58.610 --> 00:15:59.990
But I tell this story
because it highlights

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:02.210
the first impactful
lesson from the military

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:04.470
that I wanted to share with you,

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:06.100
is the power of your 'why'

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:08.140
and how important it is
to not only identify it,

00:16:08.140 --> 00:16:10.130
but to never lose sight of it.

00:16:10.130 --> 00:16:12.730
Never once, did I consider
quitting in those weeks

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:16.430
when your why is strong
enough, the how is irrelevant.

00:16:16.430 --> 00:16:18.247
as Viktor Frankl said in his book,

00:16:18.247 --> 00:16:19.287
"Man's Search For Meaning,

00:16:19.287 --> 00:16:21.277
"suffering ceases to be suffering

00:16:21.277 --> 00:16:23.210
"at the moment it finds a meaning."

00:16:23.210 --> 00:16:25.327
My "why" in Ranger School,

00:16:25.327 --> 00:16:26.947
"Remember what you promised your family,

00:16:26.947 --> 00:16:28.567
"yourself and your brothers."

00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:30.283
That was my "Why".

00:16:30.283 --> 00:16:31.310
I wrote that sentence in Sharpie,

00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:33.510
underneath the brim of my patrol cap.

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:35.130
So that every time I was
feeling sorry for myself

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:37.320
in a low point, I get down on a knee,

00:16:37.320 --> 00:16:39.530
remove the cap from my head
and read it to remind myself

00:16:39.530 --> 00:16:40.980
why I had to keep going,

00:16:40.980 --> 00:16:41.990
over the next three months,

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:44.440
blood, sweat, and dirt
was stained into that cap.

00:16:45.310 --> 00:16:46.143
And when I finished the course,

00:16:46.143 --> 00:16:48.070
I gave it to my father who
still has it to this day.

00:16:48.070 --> 00:16:50.290
The writing on it is blurred
and almost illegible,

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:51.360
but it can be read.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.600
Often, your "Why" may be hard to see,

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:56.110
but I promise you it's there,
if you look hard enough.

00:16:56.110 --> 00:16:58.607
The second lesson for my
service that I'll share,

00:16:58.607 --> 00:17:00.357
"Do something difficult every day."

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:03.530
Why would I give so much during training?

00:17:03.530 --> 00:17:05.820
I mean, this was just a
Ranger School after all,

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:07.050
it wasn't Afghanistan.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:09.837
But that's what Special
Operations emphasizes,

00:17:09.837 --> 00:17:12.953
"How you do small things
is how you do all things."

00:17:12.953 --> 00:17:14.320
It's important to learn about who you are

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:16.750
at your lowest point,
to test your character,

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:18.480
when at rock bottom.

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:19.920
In Ranger School, I was constantly in pain

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:22.290
and asking myself the
question many of us do

00:17:22.290 --> 00:17:23.837
when we were facing something difficult.

00:17:23.837 --> 00:17:25.760
"How long will this last?"

00:17:25.760 --> 00:17:28.520
Instead, I encourage you
to ask, "How far can I go?"

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:31.090
If I couldn't push myself further then,

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:33.670
how could I be expected to push
further when I was deployed,

00:17:33.670 --> 00:17:36.090
when there was much more
in the line in combat.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:37.870
The only way I could
truly learn about myself

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:39.490
and how far I could really go,

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:41.230
was by doing something
difficult every day,

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:43.690
and that was life and Ranger Regiment.

00:17:43.690 --> 00:17:45.730
However, I'll admit that
was a different environment.

00:17:45.730 --> 00:17:47.340
It was easier for me to choose difficult

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:50.370
because there was much more
at stake than just myself,

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:52.470
after all that was preparation for combat.

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:56.620
It is far, far easier to
choose what it's easy,

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:58.270
when things don't go the way
where they're supposed to,

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:01.000
it is human nature to
put the blame elsewhere.

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:01.833
So in these moments,

00:18:01.833 --> 00:18:04.250
I challenge you to look
deep within yourself.

00:18:04.250 --> 00:18:06.520
Life is pain in struggle,
it's inevitable in life

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:10.250
that you'll find yourself
facing bad situations,

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:12.140
but it's how you face these difficulties

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:13.640
that will determine your fate.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:17.870
Your obstacles are not these
things, events, or people,

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:19.040
the obstacle is yourself.

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:20.820
See the role you played in any failure,

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:22.470
it can always be found.

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:23.497
As Viktor Frankl wrote,

00:18:23.497 --> 00:18:26.057
"When we are no longer able
to change the situation,

00:18:26.057 --> 00:18:27.697
"we are challenged to change ourselves,

00:18:27.697 --> 00:18:30.437
"so choose difficult, you
will learn more about yourself

00:18:30.437 --> 00:18:31.857
"and will be better for it."

00:18:33.070 --> 00:18:35.340
When I left the Ranger
Regiment and came to Yale,

00:18:35.340 --> 00:18:37.720
the transition was very difficult.

00:18:37.720 --> 00:18:38.610
At the end of my first year,

00:18:38.610 --> 00:18:40.280
I wrote an article describing
some of my thoughts

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:42.840
as a veteran who recently
left the service.

00:18:42.840 --> 00:18:45.350
Initially I did not want to publish it,

00:18:45.350 --> 00:18:47.610
but I realized that it wasn't about me.

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:49.090
I realized that I was fortunate

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:52.280
to be part of an incredible
institution here at Yale,

00:18:52.280 --> 00:18:54.360
privileged to have a platform

00:18:54.360 --> 00:18:56.010
that could reach a large audience

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:58.470
and that my article could serve as a voice

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:00.920
for all the veterans out
there who don't have one.

00:19:00.920 --> 00:19:04.000
The veterans who may not want
to or may not know how to,

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:07.580
directly express their thoughts
to their friends and family,

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:08.870
and just maybe they could possibly share

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:10.670
this article with them instead.

00:19:10.670 --> 00:19:12.530
If I somehow was able to
reach this one veteran

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:15.080
with that message, that
would make it all worth it.

00:19:16.040 --> 00:19:17.217
In the article I said that,

00:19:17.217 --> 00:19:21.330
"Civilians did not need to
understand us to care about us."

00:19:21.330 --> 00:19:23.300
However, after some thought,

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:24.820
I do believe that asking others to care,

00:19:24.820 --> 00:19:27.040
maybe a bit of an overstep.

00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:28.660
I sit alongside peers

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.242
that don't truly comprehend
the events of 911.

00:19:31.242 --> 00:19:34.790
War is a far away concept,
blurred by distance,

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:36.293
experienced through screens.

00:19:37.250 --> 00:19:40.060
Skin in the game, is one thing
that goes through my mind,

00:19:40.060 --> 00:19:42.020
every time I have to
evaluate someone's motivation

00:19:42.020 --> 00:19:45.110
on a matter, their capacity to truly care.

00:19:45.110 --> 00:19:47.400
See today, unlike previous generations,

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:49.370
far fewer individuals
have a direct connection

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:50.533
to military service.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:54.160
We have far fewer people who
have ever worn the uniform,

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:56.080
who've ever experienced from
the firsthand perspective

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:58.150
of what it means to serve in sacrifice.

00:19:58.150 --> 00:20:00.360
But whether they've
experienced war or not,

00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:02.920
it's important to understand
that veterans willingness

00:20:02.920 --> 00:20:07.003
to sacrifice everything
cannot be forgotten.

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:09.330
It must be honored.

00:20:09.330 --> 00:20:10.980
I believe that there was
much progress to be made

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:14.630
if individuals seek to try
to understand their veterans.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:17.730
So I will conclude this speech
by trying to do just that,

00:20:17.730 --> 00:20:19.950
helping others understand their veterans,

00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:21.750
even if it's just a tiny bit better.

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:27.710
Many veterans spent large
portions of their lives,

00:20:27.710 --> 00:20:29.850
give their youths to
fight for the generations

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:30.900
that come after them.

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:33.400
They risk their lives hoping to make way

00:20:33.400 --> 00:20:34.680
for a better tomorrow,

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:36.200
so that those who are not yet born

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:38.880
would have the opportunity
to live in a better world.

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:40.730
Some of us matured quicker

00:20:40.730 --> 00:20:44.160
because the world showed us
it's dark side at a young age.

00:20:44.160 --> 00:20:46.020
We trained and fought together,

00:20:46.020 --> 00:20:48.600
regardless of any differences among us,

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:50.550
these were our brothers.

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:51.670
Brotherhood had nothing to do with

00:20:51.670 --> 00:20:53.470
how we felt about each other.

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:55.100
It didn't matter what we
thought about each other,

00:20:55.100 --> 00:20:57.590
or if we would hang out with
each other outside of work,

00:20:57.590 --> 00:21:00.060
but when it came down to it,
we would die for each other.

00:21:00.060 --> 00:21:01.500
That's brotherhood.

00:21:01.500 --> 00:21:02.870
We serve for similar reasons.

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:05.570
We signed up and fought for
those that we love back home,

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:08.000
fellow American citizens
that we did not know,

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.470
and for those oppressed in a foreign land.

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:11.670
It didn't matter what race they were,

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:12.970
or what religion they believed in,

00:21:12.970 --> 00:21:15.070
or what side of politics they supported.

00:21:15.070 --> 00:21:16.950
None of that mattered
when we were out there,

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:18.757
what mattered was the man
to our left and right,

00:21:18.757 --> 00:21:22.153
and the core values that we
were willing to die to protect.

00:21:24.560 --> 00:21:27.590
We're not invincible, as much
as we used to like to think

00:21:27.590 --> 00:21:30.770
and convince ourselves that
we were, war humbled us.

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:32.600
We had this idea from the media,

00:21:32.600 --> 00:21:34.370
or video games, or the recruiter,

00:21:34.370 --> 00:21:36.370
on what would be so glorious about it.

00:21:36.370 --> 00:21:38.410
But once that protective bubble burst,

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:39.570
and it suddenly sat in

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:41.670
that death could very
much become a reality,

00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:43.940
it was a humbling experience.

00:21:43.940 --> 00:21:45.810
We don't particularly
miss the war on combat,

00:21:45.810 --> 00:21:47.730
we missed the brotherhood, the camaraderie,

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:49.930
knowing exactly what we do for each other

00:21:49.930 --> 00:21:52.993
without hesitation, that
is what we miss the most.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:56.700
Many veterans struggled with depression

00:21:56.700 --> 00:21:57.990
when they return home,

00:21:57.990 --> 00:21:59.620
not because they long
for the pity of others,

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:02.520
but because they are searching
again for a purpose, a "Why."

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:03.890
When we're over there,

00:22:03.890 --> 00:22:06.920
we dreamt about how amazing
it would be to go home.

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:07.753
But when we get home,

00:22:07.753 --> 00:22:09.750
we realize that life
carried on without us,

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:11.900
that we were never really needed here.

00:22:11.900 --> 00:22:13.880
Veterans returned to what seems like

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:18.020
a bubble of access materialism,
cynicism, and ingratitude,

00:22:18.020 --> 00:22:19.460
and find themselves stripped to the roles

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:21.240
they'd spent years of their lives

00:22:21.240 --> 00:22:22.960
devoting their blood and sweat to.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:25.930
Over there people relied
on us, we were needed.

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:28.550
Even if it wasn't what we
initially volunteered for,

00:22:28.550 --> 00:22:31.250
it was outside of this country
that we find a purpose.

00:22:32.520 --> 00:22:35.210
Military personnel are like a strong seal.

00:22:35.210 --> 00:22:36.830
They endure a heavy amount of pressure

00:22:36.830 --> 00:22:39.500
and showing no external
signs of stress or damage

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:41.270
until they just break.

00:22:41.270 --> 00:22:42.190
From personal experience,

00:22:42.190 --> 00:22:43.610
I can say that the military teaches you

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:46.010
to separate your mind from your emotions.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:47.730
You cannot survive in combat

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:49.610
unless you've mastered this task.

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:51.820
Emotional thinking can
lead to second guessing,

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:53.960
second guessing can lead to hesitation,

00:22:53.960 --> 00:22:56.890
and hesitation could
lead to injury or death.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:58.380
Therefore, this ability is like a switch

00:22:58.380 --> 00:23:00.610
that one must flip on and off.

00:23:00.610 --> 00:23:03.740
It spills over to one's
personal life and relationships.

00:23:03.740 --> 00:23:05.140
Then you were told that
you were cold-hearted

00:23:05.140 --> 00:23:07.600
because you no longer
express yourself emotionally,

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:09.610
and you begin to drift apart.

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:10.820
Then it seems that the only people

00:23:10.820 --> 00:23:12.210
that understand or relate to you

00:23:12.210 --> 00:23:14.990
are those with which you served.

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:17.320
You find yourself closer to
them than your actual family,

00:23:17.320 --> 00:23:19.860
your spouse, your civilian friends.

00:23:19.860 --> 00:23:21.150
It can lead to a very difficult time

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:22.693
adjusting to civilian norms.

00:23:24.160 --> 00:23:25.310
In the military and combat

00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:27.230
it's imperative to exist without emotion,

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:30.260
yet, emotions are what we
are genetically programmed

00:23:30.260 --> 00:23:33.210
to express and display in
everyday civilian life.

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:35.420
That is what makes the life of a veteran

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:36.433
extremely difficult.

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:41.050
Everyone seems to love as
soldier until they come home.

00:23:41.050 --> 00:23:43.240
They don't ask us about our
experiences for the most part,

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:45.289
not because they don't want to disturb us,

00:23:45.289 --> 00:23:48.240
but it's because they're
afraid of being disturbed

00:23:48.240 --> 00:23:51.852
by what it takes for
them to be safe and free.

00:23:51.852 --> 00:23:55.853
We sleep sound under a
warm blanket of freedom,

00:23:57.320 --> 00:23:58.770
unbothered and not knowing

00:23:58.770 --> 00:24:01.750
that these rough individuals will do,

00:24:01.750 --> 00:24:05.070
and go to the farthest ends to protect us.

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:06.300
The thought that freedom and safety

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:07.480
is not a given birthright

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:10.363
is an uncomfortable truth
to the normal civilian.

00:24:11.320 --> 00:24:13.100
You may wonder why that's
so surprising to you

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:15.250
that some combat veterans are lost here

00:24:15.250 --> 00:24:17.403
and want to actually go back to war.

00:24:17.403 --> 00:24:20.640
A reason to consider is that
it's a selfless society in war

00:24:20.640 --> 00:24:24.240
and comparatively, civilian
life is individualistic.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.700
Soldiers who are deploy as a cohesive unit

00:24:26.700 --> 00:24:28.500
come back to society of individuals

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:29.963
who do not behave as a unit.

00:24:31.000 --> 00:24:33.500
Most relationships that
people have with each other

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:35.210
nowadays are shallow.

00:24:35.210 --> 00:24:36.940
Most don't know their neighbors.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:38.800
People in universities often spend years

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:40.230
seeing each other in lectures

00:24:40.230 --> 00:24:43.120
and never come to
genuinely know each other.

00:24:43.120 --> 00:24:44.640
With technology and social media,

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:46.980
our generation has been
trained to be isolated

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:49.400
from human contact and
each day this creates

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:51.513
a more isolated situation for each.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:56.360
As citizens, we are
responsible for a society.

00:24:56.360 --> 00:24:58.350
We must strive to have respect for things,

00:24:58.350 --> 00:25:00.240
for people that we do not understand.

00:25:00.240 --> 00:25:02.890
We must not be individuals in society

00:25:02.890 --> 00:25:04.710
who seek echo chambers,

00:25:04.710 --> 00:25:06.880
we self categorize and tribalize,

00:25:06.880 --> 00:25:07.720
because what this does

00:25:07.720 --> 00:25:09.723
is breaks apart the American tapestry.

00:25:10.890 --> 00:25:12.410
You must not fail to see the big picture

00:25:12.410 --> 00:25:13.850
and remember that there are things

00:25:13.850 --> 00:25:15.240
much more important than ourselves,

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:18.470
such as maintaining respect
for differing opinions

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:19.850
and remaining United as a nation,

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:23.310
because it is a selfless
respectful thing to do,

00:25:23.310 --> 00:25:25.610
because that matters
more to the greater good.

00:25:26.940 --> 00:25:28.380
We must not become preoccupied

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:30.510
with attempting to
dictate change to society

00:25:30.510 --> 00:25:33.500
before looking inside ourselves first.

00:25:33.500 --> 00:25:35.640
Thus, if we were more introspective

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:38.600
and less determined to bend
the world to our ideology,

00:25:38.600 --> 00:25:41.350
we would by definition, be more unified.

00:25:41.350 --> 00:25:42.640
The question for each one of us is,

00:25:42.640 --> 00:25:44.023
how do we begin to unite?

00:25:44.940 --> 00:25:45.990
Humility and perspective

00:25:45.990 --> 00:25:48.400
will be our greatest
teachers moving forward.

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.870
Keep humility close to your
heart and maintain perspective,

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:53.770
and together we can solve so
many more challenges in life.

00:25:54.760 --> 00:25:58.243
Humility and perspective allow
us to learn from each other.

00:25:59.370 --> 00:26:03.480
I speak to you, to my fellow
veterans you are among

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:06.200
some of the best trained
individuals in the world.

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:08.300
You underwent a transformation as I have

00:26:08.300 --> 00:26:09.750
in order to stay vigilant and be alive

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:10.970
in a place that prides itself

00:26:10.970 --> 00:26:13.190
on taking you out without cause.

00:26:13.190 --> 00:26:14.830
You will never be the same,

00:26:14.830 --> 00:26:16.630
but the truth is you will be better.

00:26:17.900 --> 00:26:19.270
Some days will be harder than others,

00:26:19.270 --> 00:26:21.540
but the piece will come in time.

00:26:21.540 --> 00:26:23.380
One day you'll realize
just how far you've come

00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:26.470
and the struggles you face
won't feel so intimidating

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:29.760
because you realize you've
conquered so many before.

00:26:29.760 --> 00:26:31.010
Even if the pain is still there,

00:26:31.010 --> 00:26:35.120
you realize it may always
be, and somehow, that's okay.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:36.127
As Victor Frankel wrote,

00:26:36.127 --> 00:26:39.057
"What is to give light
must endure burning."

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.940
When a bone breaks and aligns
incorrectly in your body

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:44.470
a doctor will often have to re-break it

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:46.170
in order for you to heal properly,

00:26:47.390 --> 00:26:49.440
the same is true for the ego.

00:26:49.440 --> 00:26:51.460
Past trauma or the
circumstance of the world

00:26:51.460 --> 00:26:53.860
can cause our mind to align incorrectly,

00:26:53.860 --> 00:26:55.763
this healing may require a breaking.

00:26:56.900 --> 00:26:59.930
I say to you, it will
always be okay in the end,

00:26:59.930 --> 00:27:02.930
if it's not okay, it's not the end.

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:04.970
Think back to a year ago,

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:06.320
do you remember what was bothering you?

00:27:06.320 --> 00:27:08.010
Is it still bothering you?

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:09.980
What you were going through will pass.

00:27:09.980 --> 00:27:12.983
Use hindsight is foresight,
you'll be grateful for it.

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:16.393
When we honor Veterans Day,
we remember our veterans,

00:27:16.393 --> 00:27:18.910
it's important for us to
remember the incalculable debt

00:27:18.910 --> 00:27:22.090
that is owed to you, to our veterans,

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:25.290
a debt that's impossible
to ever repay fully,

00:27:25.290 --> 00:27:27.220
but it is a debt that is essential for us

00:27:27.220 --> 00:27:28.400
to always remember,

00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:30.930
to never forget the true cost of freedom,

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:33.490
and who carries the burden of that cost.

00:27:33.490 --> 00:27:36.530
As a fellow veteran, your
struggle is my struggle,

00:27:36.530 --> 00:27:39.510
your anger and frustration
are my anger and frustration.

00:27:39.510 --> 00:27:42.980
Your pain is my pain, your
challenges are my challenges.

00:27:42.980 --> 00:27:45.893
Your failures and successes
are my failures and successes.

00:27:47.420 --> 00:27:49.650
My service pales in comparison
to great individuals

00:27:49.650 --> 00:27:52.220
who've come before me,
who've paved the way,

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:54.560
to true warriors like Bobby Atkinson,

00:27:54.560 --> 00:27:56.880
Sam Myers, James Hatch.

00:27:56.880 --> 00:27:57.880
(clears throat) Excuse me.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:27:59.370
Who also attend this great university

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:01.770
and provide more value
than most will ever know.

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:05.870
I'm not a hero, I served with real heroes.

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:07.750
Those who wake up every
single day at 5:00 AM

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:09.030
and put their boots and uniform on,

00:28:09.030 --> 00:28:11.100
when nobody has any idea where they're at,

00:28:11.100 --> 00:28:12.530
or the things they're doing,

00:28:12.530 --> 00:28:14.570
and to those who can never return.

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:16.420
And for the gold star
families who carry on today

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:20.003
without sons, daughters, fathers,
and mothers, I honor you.

00:28:22.130 --> 00:28:24.280
To Staff Sergeant Jason Dockery,

00:28:24.280 --> 00:28:26.300
Private First Class Eric Hario,

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:29.800
Corporal Andrew Amesbury,
Staff Sergeant Avonye Chisolm,

00:28:29.800 --> 00:28:31.833
Sergeant First Class Christopher Celiz,

00:28:32.670 --> 00:28:34.230
Specialist Etienne Murphy,

00:28:34.230 --> 00:28:35.930
Sergeant First Class Ethan Carpenter,

00:28:35.930 --> 00:28:37.430
these are just a few great men

00:28:38.410 --> 00:28:41.760
who serve as part of my reason
why today and every day,

00:28:41.760 --> 00:28:43.930
to all the veterans who have served,

00:28:43.930 --> 00:28:45.460
thank you for your sacrifices you've made

00:28:45.460 --> 00:28:47.030
and continued to make.

00:28:47.030 --> 00:28:48.930
You have given this country a privilege

00:28:48.930 --> 00:28:50.223
that's beyond payment.

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:55.200
We honor you for that today
and every day, thank you.

00:28:55.200 --> 00:28:58.309
(crowd claps)

00:28:58.309 --> 00:29:01.559
(Color Guard commands)

00:29:21.354 --> 00:29:23.437
("Taps")

00:30:28.160 --> 00:30:29.040
- (sighs) I don't know about you,

00:30:29.040 --> 00:30:30.680
I feel the need to just
kind of like breathe

00:30:30.680 --> 00:30:34.030
for just a second, that was
very, very impactful speech,

00:30:34.030 --> 00:30:37.033
Andrew, thank you very much as well.

00:30:38.580 --> 00:30:40.790
From time to time, the
university recognizes

00:30:40.790 --> 00:30:43.270
outstanding contributions to veterans,

00:30:43.270 --> 00:30:45.143
with the Veterans Day Tribute Award,

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:48.600
and this year is my great
honor to present the award

00:30:48.600 --> 00:30:51.330
to Valerie Stanley, senior director

00:30:51.330 --> 00:30:54.273
of the office of institutional
equity and accessibility.

00:30:55.338 --> 00:30:56.900
Now in a moment, Valerie
will make her way here

00:30:56.900 --> 00:30:58.940
so we can actually give her award,

00:30:58.940 --> 00:30:59.773
but actually is when I want to tell you

00:30:59.773 --> 00:31:01.560
a little bit about why

00:31:01.560 --> 00:31:03.783
the committee thought
this was appropriate.

00:31:05.090 --> 00:31:08.480
Many of you may know the
Valerie has been on staff

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:11.748
for 47 years, as I mentioned,
that's like 47 years.

00:31:11.748 --> 00:31:14.250
(chuckles) And for 44 of those years,

00:31:14.250 --> 00:31:17.480
she has been involved in
crafting policy and plans

00:31:17.480 --> 00:31:19.900
to comply with federal
requirements in areas,

00:31:19.900 --> 00:31:23.519
including Title VI, the ADA, Title IX

00:31:23.519 --> 00:31:27.870
Section 504 and Age
Discrimination, to name a few.

00:31:27.870 --> 00:31:31.540
In fact, in the late 1970s,

00:31:31.540 --> 00:31:34.110
Valerie helped write the
university's first affirmative

00:31:34.110 --> 00:31:37.030
action and equal opportunity
plan for the United States

00:31:37.030 --> 00:31:39.253
Department of Health,
Education and Welfare.

00:31:40.100 --> 00:31:42.740
She worked 14 straight days

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:45.053
using typewriters and carbon paper.

00:31:45.950 --> 00:31:47.180
Now, if you don't know what that is,

00:31:47.180 --> 00:31:48.878
you can just see me later, okay?

00:31:48.878 --> 00:31:50.510
(crowd laughs)

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:52.300
But what many of you may not know

00:31:52.300 --> 00:31:55.420
is that Valerie has also
been for all of those years

00:31:55.420 --> 00:31:57.820
an outstanding and a really long-term ally

00:31:57.820 --> 00:31:58.893
for our veterans.

00:31:59.910 --> 00:32:02.350
In her early days, she
had a special affinity

00:32:02.350 --> 00:32:04.600
and affection for Vietnam veterans

00:32:04.600 --> 00:32:07.690
who often felt unwelcome on their return,

00:32:07.690 --> 00:32:09.230
and were hesitant to share their service

00:32:09.230 --> 00:32:11.540
and experience with the old colleagues.

00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:13.890
She would support them and
she continues to support them

00:32:13.890 --> 00:32:15.043
however she could.

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:20.990
In 2011, Valerie began working
with Debbie Stanley McCauley,

00:32:20.990 --> 00:32:22.420
who is the associate vice president

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:24.930
for employee engagement
and workplace culture.

00:32:24.930 --> 00:32:27.210
And she worked to
advocate for the creation

00:32:27.210 --> 00:32:30.683
of a new affinity group on
campus for staff veterans.

00:32:32.120 --> 00:32:35.700
As a result, we now have
the Yale Veterans Network,

00:32:35.700 --> 00:32:39.220
a Yale community of veterans,
veteran leaders and allies,

00:32:39.220 --> 00:32:41.880
who support and encourage each other,

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:45.060
in addition to advocating
for veteran recruitment,

00:32:45.060 --> 00:32:48.350
career development, growth and retention.

00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:50.440
And today that network,

00:32:50.440 --> 00:32:53.090
the Yale Veterans Network is thriving

00:32:53.090 --> 00:32:55.070
and Valerie serves and continues to serve

00:32:55.070 --> 00:32:56.370
on its steering committee.

00:32:58.010 --> 00:33:00.290
In addition to supporting these veterans,

00:33:00.290 --> 00:33:03.120
Valerie has made a point to
help all students in particular

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:05.063
understand the veteran experience.

00:33:06.820 --> 00:33:09.240
Valerie, you're going
to retire in December

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:11.033
after 47 years at Yale,

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:13.690
but before you go,

00:33:13.690 --> 00:33:16.320
I am so pleased to present
you with this award

00:33:16.320 --> 00:33:19.560
on behalf of the Veterans Day
committee and the university

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:21.500
in recognition of your support,

00:33:21.500 --> 00:33:24.833
your longterm support
for veterans at Yale,

00:33:26.100 --> 00:33:29.720
please join me and
accepting this small token

00:33:29.720 --> 00:33:33.416
of our admiration and respect (mumbles)

00:33:33.416 --> 00:33:34.890
and your contributions.

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:36.168
Thank you very, very much.

00:33:36.168 --> 00:33:40.835
(crowd claps)
(mumbles)

00:33:50.434 --> 00:33:51.950
- Thank you Kim and thank you everyone.

00:33:51.950 --> 00:33:53.730
I don't have a prepared speech,

00:33:53.730 --> 00:33:58.730
but I do want to just
express my sincere thanks

00:33:58.830 --> 00:34:02.670
to the veterans committee
for choosing me today.

00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:04.480
I'm not a veteran, I don't have a story.

00:34:04.480 --> 00:34:07.370
Andrew has the perfect, wonderful story,

00:34:07.370 --> 00:34:10.930
and all of the rest of you
who have served this country

00:34:10.930 --> 00:34:14.180
have equally important
and wonderful stories.

00:34:14.180 --> 00:34:17.250
Even if you haven't had the
opportunity to share them

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:18.540
with anybody else.

00:34:18.540 --> 00:34:20.620
Maybe you have with your
family, may be no one,

00:34:20.620 --> 00:34:22.960
maybe it's just something
that's in your head,

00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:24.850
but your stories are all important.

00:34:24.850 --> 00:34:27.560
Everything you've done is important.

00:34:27.560 --> 00:34:30.580
All I've tried to do is to support you

00:34:30.580 --> 00:34:32.210
in the best way that I could,

00:34:32.210 --> 00:34:34.470
in the best way that I know how,

00:34:34.470 --> 00:34:36.880
and I am truly, truly honored

00:34:37.720 --> 00:34:40.667
to accept this award from you all

00:34:40.667 --> 00:34:44.880
and this recognition after I
was only two when I started.

00:34:44.880 --> 00:34:49.510
So, (chuckles) that's my
story, I'm sticking to it.

00:34:49.510 --> 00:34:51.120
Those of you who know me for a while now,

00:34:51.120 --> 00:34:52.963
I've had that story for a long time.

00:34:53.960 --> 00:34:55.780
But anyway, thank you all so much.

00:34:55.780 --> 00:34:58.410
It is truly an honor and a pleasure.

00:34:58.410 --> 00:35:01.180
I'm going to miss you all,
but I live in New Haven,

00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:04.020
I'll be around and I'm sure
I'll see some of you around.

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:06.787
Thank you.
(crowd claps)

00:35:17.345 --> 00:35:21.012
("The Armed Forces Medley")

00:38:23.990 --> 00:38:26.490
(crowd claps)

00:38:35.353 --> 00:38:37.903
- I invite you to stand
please for The Benediction,

00:38:43.320 --> 00:38:47.040
Let us go forth from this
place of profound memory

00:38:47.040 --> 00:38:50.790
and deep meaning with grateful hearts.

00:38:50.790 --> 00:38:54.280
Grateful for the service and
sacrifice of our veterans

00:38:54.280 --> 00:38:56.170
and their families.

00:38:56.170 --> 00:38:58.260
Let us never forget all they have done

00:38:58.260 --> 00:39:01.883
to bravely protect
freedom and human rights.

00:39:02.930 --> 00:39:05.970
From the moment our veterans
made the commitment to serve

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:08.200
they were committing to a life

00:39:08.200 --> 00:39:10.333
that would be forever changed.

00:39:11.420 --> 00:39:14.560
May we be ever mindful
that the life of a veteran

00:39:14.560 --> 00:39:17.000
is always multi-dimensional

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:21.020
and is rooted in the pride
and promise of liberation

00:39:21.020 --> 00:39:24.010
coupled with the ever-present possibility

00:39:24.010 --> 00:39:26.860
of peril or alienation.

00:39:26.860 --> 00:39:29.890
All of these things are held intention

00:39:29.890 --> 00:39:33.700
and contain a longing for
a certain kind of peace

00:39:33.700 --> 00:39:36.143
to revive our weary world.

00:39:37.180 --> 00:39:39.430
As we honor our veterans

00:39:39.430 --> 00:39:43.620
and we seek to grow closer
to this abiding peace,

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:45.570
may we come to know

00:39:45.570 --> 00:39:48.653
a peace born from wisdom
and understanding,

00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:53.730
a peace nurtured with
compassion and mercy,

00:39:53.730 --> 00:39:58.060
a peace strengthened by
courage and commitment.

00:39:58.060 --> 00:40:01.500
And may we be blessed
this day and every day

00:40:01.500 --> 00:40:05.330
by the shimmering light
of our God of many names,

00:40:05.330 --> 00:40:10.150
our God who creates, sustains
and embraces all life,

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:12.323
for this we say, Amen.

00:40:21.010 --> 00:40:24.260
(Color Guard commands)

00:40:25.910 --> 00:40:27.647
- Right turn, march!

00:40:30.712 --> 00:40:31.879
Forward march!

00:40:44.700 --> 00:40:47.080
- Well, this concludes our ceremony.

00:40:47.080 --> 00:40:49.780
And with passed and
return to our busy life,

00:40:49.780 --> 00:40:51.580
I hope that we will continue

00:40:51.580 --> 00:40:53.360
to remember the women and men of Yale

00:40:53.360 --> 00:40:57.200
who have faithfully taken
oath to protect and serve.

00:40:57.200 --> 00:40:58.710
Remember that they are the reason

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:00.170
why we can pursue our "why's"

00:41:00.170 --> 00:41:03.790
and why we can build a better community.

00:41:03.790 --> 00:41:07.740
We are stronger and diverse
community because of them.

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:09.540
Many thanks to the planning committee

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:12.080
and our ceremony participants as well,

00:41:12.080 --> 00:41:13.380
A special thanks to Tom Duffy

00:41:13.380 --> 00:41:15.880
and the Yale School of
Music Brass Ensemble,

00:41:15.880 --> 00:41:19.120
for sharing with us,
moving inspiring music.

00:41:19.120 --> 00:41:20.901
Thank you for all that you do.

00:41:20.901 --> 00:41:22.703
(crowd claps)

00:41:22.703 --> 00:41:24.703
Thank you, everybody and enjoy your day.

